$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=- x\cdot 2 z+0\\&=- 2 x z
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. Use the constant multiple and chain rules, then substitute the coordinates. At this point the radicand is $9>0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}&=y\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x^{2} + 4 z^{2}}}\cdot 2 x\\&=\frac{x y}{\sqrt{x^{2} + 4 z^{2}}}\\\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\big|_{(0,-1,\frac{3}{2})}&=\frac{1(0)(-1)}{\sqrt{9}}\\&=0
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. Use the constant multiple and chain rules, then substitute the coordinates. At this point the radicand is $25>0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=x\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{4 y^{2} + z^{2}}}\cdot 2 z\\&=\frac{x z}{\sqrt{4 y^{2} + z^{2}}}\\\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}\big|_{(-1,-2,3)}&=\frac{1(3)(-1)}{\sqrt{25}}\\&=- \frac{3}{5}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate with respect to $z$, holding $x$ and $y$ fixed. Apply the sum rule. In each term, differentiate only the factors that depend on $z$; all remaining factors stay fixed.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial f}{\partial z}&=\left(- 2 x - 2 y\right)\cos z+- 2 \cos{\left(y \right)}\cdot 1\\&=\left(- 2 x - 2 y\right) \cos{\left(z \right)} - 2 \cos{\left(y \right)}
\end{aligned}
$$

