question.:question/problem:
Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y)$, given that

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x,y)&=e^{- x + 2 y}, \\ g(x,y)&=- x^{2} + 2 y^{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

question.:question/worked-solution:
Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

First find the partial derivatives, holding other coordinates constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{x}&=- e^{- x + 2 y} \\ g_{x}&=- 2 x \\ f_{y}&=2 e^{- x + 2 y} \\ g_{y}&=4 y
\end{aligned}
$$

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(- x^{2} + 2 y^{2})(- e^{- x + 2 y}) \\ &\quad+(e^{- x + 2 y})(- 2 x) \\ &=\left(x^{2} - 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- x + 2 y} \\ &\quad+(- 2 x e^{- x + 2 y}) \\ &=\left(x^{2} - 2 x - 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- x + 2 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(- x^{2} + 2 y^{2})(2 e^{- x + 2 y}) \\ &\quad+(e^{- x + 2 y})(4 y) \\ &=2 \left(- x^{2} + 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- x + 2 y} \\ &\quad+(4 y e^{- x + 2 y}) \\ &=2 \left(- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2 y\right) e^{- x + 2 y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the gradient is

$$
\begin{aligned}&\left(x^{2} - 2 x - 2 y^{2}\right) e^{- x + 2 y}\mathbf i \\ &{}+2 \left(- x^{2} + 2 y^{2} + 2 y\right) e^{- x + 2 y}\mathbf j\end{aligned}
$$

Collect the contributions to each coordinate separately.

