question.:question/worked-solution:
Use the **product rule**: $\nabla(fg)=g\nabla f+f\nabla g$. Multiply each gradient by the other scalar function.

First find the partial derivatives, holding other coordinates constant:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f_{x}&=- e^{- y} \\ g_{x}&=2 y e^{2 x} \\ f_{y}&=x e^{- y} \\ g_{y}&=e^{2 x}
\end{aligned}
$$

When combining exponential factors, use $e^u e^v=e^{u+v}$ with the exponents shown in the question.

Now collect each component:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{x}&=g f_{x}+f g_{x} \\ &=(y e^{2 x})(- e^{- y}) \\ &\quad+(- x e^{- y})(2 y e^{2 x}) \\ &=- y e^{2 x} e^{- y} \\ &\quad+(- 2 x y e^{2 x} e^{- y}) \\ &=- y \left(2 x + 1\right) e^{2 x} e^{- y}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla(fg))_{y}&=g f_{y}+f g_{y} \\ &=(y e^{2 x})(x e^{- y}) \\ &\quad+(- x e^{- y})(e^{2 x}) \\ &=x y e^{2 x} e^{- y} \\ &\quad+(- x e^{2 x} e^{- y}) \\ &=x \left(y - 1\right) e^{2 x} e^{- y}
