Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=2 t^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=4 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(2 \left(2 t^{2}\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=4 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(2 \left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(4 t\right) \\ &=8 t^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{0}^{2}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{0}^{2}\left(12 t^{2}\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[4 t^{3}\right]_{0}^{2} \\ &=\left(32\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=32
\end{aligned}
$$

Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.

$$
x(t)=t,\qquad y(t)=2 t^{2}.
$$

The stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives

$$
x'(t)=1,\qquad y'(t)=4 t.
$$

Write the coefficients of $\mathrm{d}x$ and $\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
A(t)&=\left(\left(t\right)\right)\cdot \left(1\right) \\ &=t
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
B(t)&=\left(- \left(2 t^{2}\right)\right)\cdot \left(4 t\right) \\ &=- 8 t^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

The differential form uses the sum of these contributions:

$$
\begin{aligned}
I&=\int_{0}^{1}\left(A(t)+B(t)\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\int_{0}^{1}\left(- 8 t^{3} + t\right)\,\mathrm{d}t \\ &=\left[- 2 t^{4} + \frac{t^{2}}{2}\right]_{0}^{1} \\ &=\left(- \frac{3}{2}\right)-\left(0\right) \\ &=- \frac{3}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

